I am a software engineer and I am tasked to manually find logical errors in a
lot of code, if any exist. You need to help me get this done faster.

### Task

The task is to examine the logic of the code and find where in the code
possible logical bugs exist in the target function. Then, for each bug I must
insert a new if-statement just before the bug occurs. The condition must
evaluate to "True" ONLY WHEN the bug is triggered and the program MUST call a
function called "funlogline();". If no such condition can be created, insert a
new statement ("funlogline();") one line before the bug. Only modify the target
function specified at the beginning.

### Formatting

1. I would like you to format your answers only as a list of the lines of code
you think the logical bugs exist, then for each line use the following format:
"file name:function name:line number". After, insert another page-break, and
only then you may insert your reasons if any but keep them brief. If the
filename is not provided then leave it explicitly blank.

2. For each identified logical bug: modify the corresponding function by
inserting a new if statement which calls "funlogline();" if the conditions for
this bug to trigger are met. Format the modifications as a single file in
unified diff format.

### Task notes

More notes on your task:
- obfuscated or non-descriptive function are not logical errors. They might lead to more logical errors from the user's perspective but they are not pertinent to this analysis. A warning about this is fine if appended at the end of all the reasoning provided by you.
- the following are not logical errors: double-free; use-after-free; out of bounds access; memory leaks.
- pointer casts are not logical errors.
- do not "clean up partial state"
- do not create new functions that would solve current issues
- do not modify or suggest comment modifications
- you may use context and the call-graph to infer context

### Examples

= Example 1

Target function: "_strstr(const char* str, const char *needle)"

---- File: grep.c

/* Does some cool stuff with grepping and matching needles.
 * */
/// Line: 7
const char *_strstr(const char* str, const char *needle) {
    size_t needle_len = strlen(needle);
    size_t str_len = strlen(str);

    for (int i = 0; i < str_len; ++i) {
        if (str_len - i < needle_len) {
            break;
        }

        // If match first check rest
        if (str[i] == needle[i]) { // <-- EXAMPLE BUG HERE
            if (strncmp(&str[i], needle, needle_len) == 0) {
                return &str[i];
            }
        }

    }

    return NULL;
}

/* Helps with sleep 
 * */
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc < 3) {
        printf("We require arguments: <PAT> <FILE>\n");
    }

    char *needle = strdup(argv[1]);

    FILE* file = fopen(argv[2], "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        perror("No file");
        exit(1);
    }

    char *line = NULL;
    size_t line_len = 0;
    while (getline(&line, &line_len, file) != -1) {
        if (_strstr(line, needle) != NULL) {
            printf("%s", line);
        }
        free(line);
        line = NULL;
    }

    free(needle);
    free(line);
    fclose(file);
}

